threads.body:
I think the only point of GenericPart is to provide extensibility and forward compatibility, i.e. it should allow to record provider-specific parts and should set expectation for consumers that anything else except known types is expected.

So I think GenericPart should not have a fixed discriminator value, but should support any unknown one. 

AI is telling in can be done with something like 

```python
from typing import get_args
from pydantic import Discriminator, Tag, field_validator

_KNOWN_PARTS = (
    TextPart, ToolCallRequestPart, ToolCallResponsePart, ServerToolCallPart,
    ServerToolCallResponsePart, BlobPart, FilePart, UriPart, ReasoningPart, CompactionPart,
)
KNOWN_PART_TYPES = tuple(get_args(p.model_fields["type"].annotation)[0] for p in _KNOWN_PARTS)


class GenericPart(BaseModel):
    type: str = Field(
        description="The type of the content captured in this part.",
        json_schema_extra={"not": {"enum": list(KNOWN_PART_TYPES)}},
    )
    model_config = ConfigDict(extra="allow")

    # optional: also reject GenericPart(type="text") when built directly
    @field_validator("type")
    @classmethod
    def _not_known(cls, v: str) -> str:
        if v in KNOWN_PART_TYPES:
            raise ValueError(f"'{v}' is a known part type")
        return v


def _part_tag(v: Any) -> str:
    t = v.get("type") if isinstance(v, dict) else getattr(v, "type", None)
    return t if t in KNOWN_PART_TYPES else "generic"


MessagePart = Annotated[
    Union[
        Annotated[TextPart, Tag("text")],
        Annotated[ToolCallRequestPart, Tag("tool_call")],
        Annotated[ToolCallResponsePart, Tag("tool_call_response")],
        Annotated[ServerToolCallPart, Tag("server_tool_call")],
        Annotated[ServerToolCallResponsePart, Tag("server_tool_call_response")],
        Annotated[BlobPart, Tag("blob")],
        Annotated[FilePart, Tag("file")],
        Annotated[UriPart, Tag("uri")],
        Annotated[ReasoningPart, Tag("reasoning")],
        Annotated[CompactionPart, Tag("compaction")],
        Annotated[GenericPart, Tag("generic")],
    ],
    Discriminator(_part_tag),
]

```

and translates into json schema like

```json
"MessagePart": {
  "oneOf": [
    {"$ref": "#/$defs/TextPart"},
    ...
    {"$ref": "#/$defs/GenericPart"}
  ]
},
"GenericPart": {
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "type": {"type": "string", "not": {"enum": ["text", "tool_call", "...", "compaction"]}}
  },
  "required": ["type"],
  "additionalProperties": true
}
```

replies.body:
plain old deserialization logic

```python
parts = []
for p in json.loads(raw):
    part_type = p.get("type")
    if part_type == "text":
        parts.append(TextPart(content=p["content"]))
    elif part_type == "tool_call":
        parts.append(ToolCallRequestPart(id=p.get("id"), name=p["name"], arguments=p.get("arguments")))
    elif part_type == "tool_call_response":
        parts.append(ToolCallResponsePart(id=p.get("id"), response=p.get("response")))
    else:
        parts.append(p)  # unknown type, keep as dict
```

plus guidance that instrumentations SHOULD NOT record parts they don't support and SHOULD document structure of parts they do support in the way that does not contradict core set. 

This allows instrumentations to extend standard set of parts, semantic conventions to evolve the list and tells consumers to expect unknown types.
